WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:04.270
- We added almost a half,
almost 500,000 new jobs,

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:07.040
net new jobs, total new jobs
in the state, which is a lot.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.642
We cut more taxes
than ever been cut,

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:12.679
but we also raised the
average income level

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:13.913
the fastest rate in the country.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.615 align:start size:84% position:16%
We were the fastest
improving state

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:18.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the country in
education results, K, 12.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.887
We did it in our way,

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.422
not the way that is current,

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:24.024
being proposed by the
current administration.

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:26.126
We actually made a program
where community colleges

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:29.462
and our technical schools
could be open to everyone.

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:32.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
We actually had a
free technical school

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:34.467
and community college
program for folks.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.970
So, the idea being, let's
level the playing field

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:38.705
on the front

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:42.075
so we don't have to do so much
to help folks on the backend.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:45.245
And I think we finally just
made this a type of place

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:46.746
that people want to live.

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.215
(funky music)

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.888 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:57.357 line:0%
is a visiting professor
of political science

00:00:57.357 --> 00:00:59.159 line:0%
at Vanderbilt University,

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:02.395
the former two term
governor of Tennessee

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:04.264
and author of the new book,

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:08.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Faithful Presence: The
Promise and Peril of Faith

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.970
in the Public Square,"
Governor Bill Haslam.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.672
Welcome to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.007
- Well, thanks for having me.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:16.810
It's good to be with
you and your viewers.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.611
- Well, I'm glad to have
you on because, you know,

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
we get sent an
awful lot of books.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.549 align:start size:84% position:16%
We get sent an awful
lot of pitches,

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:25.185
and I can't have everybody
on, but I wanted to do this

00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:28.488
because I thought what you're
talking about in this book,

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:33.126
the blend of politics and faith
is particularly interesting.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:34.694
I found it interesting
at a personal level,

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:35.862
just because of my own sort of

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:37.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
political and
spiritual evolution.

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:39.732 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that sort of
will be the framework

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:41.034
of where we start here.

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:42.035
But first, you're
in Tennessee now.

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:43.903
I was there a few weeks ago.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:45.405 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're doing something
right in Tennessee.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:46.906 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know you're not
the governor anymore,

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:49.943 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you must be happy with
the state of the state, right?

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:52.679 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- You know, our founders
kind of had this great idea,

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:55.081 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
like, let the states be
laboratories of democracy.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:56.616 line:0%
Let them all do
it their own way.

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:59.385 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think states like
Tennessee have chosen to be

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.755 align:start size:84% position:16%
lower tax, encouraging
business growth.

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think you're seeing
the result of that.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.126 align:start size:84% position:16%
People get to choose
where they live,

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:09.662
and we have a lot more inflow
than we do outgo these days.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.197
I'll put it that way.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:12.799
- Yeah, before we get into
the specifics of the book,

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:15.568
do you miss being in
government right now?

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.971
- You know, listen,
being in government,

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:20.140 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's such a great chance
to make a difference.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.708 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't want to sound
corny, but it, you know,

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:23.076
it's a great way to help change

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:25.111
the trajectory of
people's lives.

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:28.348
And so, I do miss that,
but I'm also somebody

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:30.517
that I kind of believe in
the whole term limit idea.

00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:32.418
Eight years of governor
is the right amount.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.120 align:start size:81% position:19%
And if you can get
something done,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
you should be able to
get it done in that time,

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:38.391
and then hand the baton to the
next person to let them run.

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:40.059 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So what were
some of the things,

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:41.227 align:start size:81% position:19%
I have a whole
list of them here,

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.529 align:start size:88% position:13%
some of the things
that you would say were

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:45.565 align:start size:88% position:13%
your big successes
when you were governor?

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:46.833
- You know, I, well, I
mean, several things.

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:50.236
I mean, I think we
added almost a half,

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:54.607 align:start size:97% position:3%
almost 500,000 new jobs,
net new jobs, total new jobs

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:55.875
in the state, which is a lot.

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:58.378
We cut more taxes
than ever been cut,

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:01.281
but we also raised the
average income level,

00:03:01.281 --> 00:03:02.649
the fastest rate in the country.

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.784 align:start size:97% position:3%
We were the fastest improving
state in the country

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:07.287
in education results, K, 12.

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:10.123
We did it in our
way, not the way

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:11.691
that is current, being proposed

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:13.193
by the current administration.

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:14.761
We actually made a program
where community colleges

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:18.298
and our technical schools
could be open to everyone.

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:21.434 align:start size:84% position:16%
We actually had a
free technical school

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:23.036
and community college
program for folks.

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:25.672
So the idea being, let's
level the playing field

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:27.273
on the front

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.642
so we don't have to do so much
to help folks on the backend.

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:33.780
And I think we finally just
made this a type of place

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:34.948
that people want to live.

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:37.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, we said to
businesses, we get it.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
Jobs get created when
people put capital at risk.

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:44.057 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think business
owners appreciated that.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.092 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You said to me right
before we started recording,

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:47.460
"Boy, you'd save a lot of money

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.563
if you move from
Cali to Tennessee."

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:54.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
What do you think it is
about blue state governors

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:58.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they can't understand
that lowering taxes

00:03:58.004 --> 00:04:01.474
actually incentivizes
business and commerce

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:05.211
and people to make decisions
for themselves, et cetera?

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:07.080 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, part of the
problem is they're stuck

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.982
with some legacy
costs that it's,

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:11.684 align:start size:97% position:3%
it takes, it's going to
take a while to work out of.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:13.620
So you have these
pension obligations

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.154
that a lot of states have

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:17.257
that you can't just snap your
fingers and make them go away.

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:18.758
They have to be addressed.

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:21.594
And I feel sorry for some
of the current governors

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.030 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it was people in
the past who promised things

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.865
that couldn't be delivered.

00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:29.068
But I honestly think a lot
of it's just not appreciation

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:31.838 line:0%
for, again, for how
jobs get created.

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:34.374 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
And folks think, well, jobs
get created by the government,

00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:37.110 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
and we know that's not
true, but you know, that's,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.345
at the end of the, for the
government to create a job,

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.814
they have to take tax money
away from folks to do that.

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:43.483 align:start size:84% position:16%
We know jobs get
created, like I said,

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.051
when people invest capital,

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:49.689
and they invest capital where
there's a more sure, you know,

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:52.959
predictor of return,
and keeping taxes low

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:54.761
is one way to do that.

00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:57.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So you were a businessman
before you were in government.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.966
So you know a little
something about this.

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:01.234
- I hope so.

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:04.771
I mean, again, you
know, I, being in,

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:06.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
people say, well, we gonna
put a business person

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:09.442 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the mayor, governor,
president's seat,

00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:11.044 align:start size:84% position:16%
and, well, it's a
different world, okay?

00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:13.646
But there's some things
you learn in business

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:17.917
that do adapt to
be in government.

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:19.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
One of those is you've got
to make the budget work

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:22.922
and you've got to have
a product, your state,

00:05:22.922 --> 00:05:24.424
you know, people get to
choose where they live,

00:05:24.424 --> 00:05:26.926
that makes it worth it for
people to choose your state.

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:29.662
- Okay, so I want to
talk about the book.

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.765 align:start position:0%
The title is "Faithful
Presence: The Promise and Peril

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:34.133
of Faith in the Public Square."

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:36.903
So first off, just
on a personal side,

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:39.305 align:start size:88% position:13%
can you tell me a little
bit about your faith?

00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:43.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, you know, back
when I was in high school,

00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:45.278
decided that the best I could,

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:48.214
I would try to follow
Jesus with my life.

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:51.317
And it's really what
led me into politics.

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:53.953
I kind of, like a lot of folks,

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:55.321
spent some time in business,

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:57.256
thought about doing a
couple of other things,

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.759
ended up being talked
into running for mayor,

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.462
and being mayor and
governor both, to me,

00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:04.664
felt like the very
best place to serve.

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:07.633 align:start size:84% position:16%
And because of that,
you know, they,

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:11.104
you talk about job as
being a calling it,

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:13.072
this, both jobs being
mayor and governor

00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:16.008
felt more like home than
anything I'd ever done.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.543
- So I think a lot of
people think, well,

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:20.513
how can you be a person of
faith and be in politics,

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:22.715
meaning politics is dirty.

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:23.983
It's messy.

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:26.018
You gotta do unethical things.

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:27.587
You gotta lie, you gotta cheat.

00:06:27.587 --> 00:06:29.756
You gotta, all the stuff.

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:32.325
How did you mitigate
all of those things?

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:35.795
And maybe the temperature
was a little bit different

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:38.765 align:start size:97% position:3%
years ago when you were
doing this, than right now.

00:06:38.765 --> 00:06:40.133 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, well, it
hasn't been that long.

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.634 align:start size:84% position:16%
Just been out of
office for two years,

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:43.669
so temperature wise, but the
temperature is definitely,

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:45.071 align:start size:81% position:19%
the temperature
continues to rise.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:46.305
You're right.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:47.407 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, I think, people say,
gosh, well is it hard

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:48.875
to be a Christian in politics?

00:06:48.875 --> 00:06:51.544
I'd say, is it a hard to
be a Christian in business,

00:06:51.544 --> 00:06:53.846
or in media or anything else?

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:56.282
Is it hard to not be
anxious about things?

00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:59.685
Is it hard to not have pride in,

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:02.622
or to have the wrong kind
of pride, to be arrogant?

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:05.658 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that the
thing about business,

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:08.327 align:start size:97% position:3%
or, thing about being in
government, I tell folks is,

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:11.030 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's a quote from Martin
Luther, the theologian,

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.133 align:start size:97% position:3%
where he said, hey, send
your very best into politics

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:17.437
because dealing with the
ambiguities of life there

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:19.305
take real insight.

00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:22.175 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, in preaching, the
Holy Spirit does all the work.

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:24.010 align:start size:81% position:19%
You have to figure
out how to live

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:25.645
in this gray world of politics.

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:27.146
And I think you can do that.

00:07:27.146 --> 00:07:28.881 align:start size:97% position:3%
And one of the things I'm
trying to encourage folks is,

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:31.951
don't be scared off from
being involved in politics,

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:33.653
from being in the public square,

00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:37.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it is such a great
place to change lives

00:07:37.657 --> 00:07:38.891
and to make a difference.

00:07:38.891 --> 00:07:40.593
- So how would you
describe the promise

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:42.094
of faith in the public square?

00:07:42.094 --> 00:07:44.964 align:start position:0%
Obviously, we have a
separation of church and state,

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:46.399 align:start size:84% position:16%
but you also have
the right to believe

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:47.533
whatever you want to believe.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:49.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
How would you
describe that promise?

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:51.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, I mean, so one of
the clear things, one of the,

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.474
again, the bright, the
incredibly smart things

00:07:54.474 --> 00:07:57.009
our founders did was say

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.478
that religion is so important,

00:07:58.478 --> 00:08:00.680
we're not going to let the
government play a role in it.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.615
So you can't, we're
not going to have

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:04.150 align:start size:84% position:16%
a government
established religion.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:05.518
But the second part of that,

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:06.752 align:start position:0%
when people talk about
separation of church and state,

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.454
it also says we're not
going to do anything

00:08:08.454 --> 00:08:10.590 align:start size:84% position:16%
to prohibit the
free exercise of it.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.859 align:start size:97% position:3%
So again, that's the
brilliance of the founders.

00:08:12.859 --> 00:08:16.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, faith is too,
religion is too important

00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:18.564
for the government
to be in charge of.

00:08:18.564 --> 00:08:21.634
And that's what I think
is really important.

00:08:21.634 --> 00:08:23.069
So, I like that.

00:08:23.069 --> 00:08:26.239 align:start position:0%
I don't want us to have a
government established religion.

00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:28.574 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, that's,
whenever you have that,

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:31.177
the church doesn't thrive
well in that environment.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:34.380
Look at Europe in that, where
the church is fairly dead,

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:38.150
where historically you had a
government established church.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:40.386
I think we do better if the
government stay out of it.

00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:43.656
But again, not to stop people
from practicing their faith

00:08:43.656 --> 00:08:45.191
in the way that they
think they should.

00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:47.493 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, are you worried
that the church in America,

00:08:47.493 --> 00:08:49.762 align:start size:88% position:13%
I would say the church,
but also temples,

00:08:49.762 --> 00:08:51.063
from a Jewish perspective.

00:08:51.063 --> 00:08:52.298
- Sure.

00:08:52.298 --> 00:08:53.866
- Probably almost
every religion,

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:55.301 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they are
becoming incredibly
politicized right now?

00:08:56.269 --> 00:08:57.203
- Well, I am.

00:08:57.203 --> 00:08:58.404
I think there's two big things.

00:08:58.404 --> 00:08:59.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
First of all, there's,
I mean, you know,

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:02.108 align:start size:81% position:19%
if you look at the
Gallup surveys,

00:09:02.108 --> 00:09:03.643
the number of people who say

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:05.811
I'm a member of a temple or
a church or place of worship

00:09:05.811 --> 00:09:08.014
has decreased dramatically.

00:09:08.014 --> 00:09:10.483 align:start position:0%
And some of that is just
cultural changes in our country.

00:09:10.483 --> 00:09:11.951
So people don't feel like, well,

00:09:11.951 --> 00:09:14.587
I have to go to temple
or I have to go to church

00:09:14.587 --> 00:09:16.856
to be a respectable
member of society.

00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:18.257
And some of that's okay.

00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:19.592 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's something we're
actually kind of, you know,

00:09:19.592 --> 00:09:21.827
sifting through
what's true faith

00:09:21.827 --> 00:09:23.896
and what is that that
I'm just doing because,

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:25.398
for appearance's sake.

00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:28.701
So I think some of
that change is good.

00:09:28.701 --> 00:09:31.003
I do worry about
what you said that

00:09:31.003 --> 00:09:34.006
we can politicize our faith.

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:36.142 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's one of the reasons
I wrote this book.

00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:39.345
The object is to be used
by God, not to use God.

00:09:39.345 --> 00:09:42.782
And if you look at
scripture, it does,

00:09:42.782 --> 00:09:44.884 align:start size:84% position:16%
God doesn't treat
lightly those people

00:09:44.884 --> 00:09:48.387
who are using Him
for other purposes.

00:09:48.387 --> 00:09:52.525
- So, as these things
get more politicized,

00:09:52.525 --> 00:09:55.061
do you sense that sort
of what has happened

00:09:55.061 --> 00:09:56.329
on the other side of the aisle,

00:09:56.329 --> 00:09:57.563
on the democratic
side of the aisle,

00:09:57.563 --> 00:10:00.099
is that there just is
no attachment to faith?

00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:02.868
I mean, they, the Democrats
used to talk about faith

00:10:02.868 --> 00:10:04.136
a little bit more.

00:10:04.136 --> 00:10:06.072
Biden sort of does,
but then, seemingly,

00:10:06.072 --> 00:10:10.376
his policies are in conflict
with the ideals of his faith.

00:10:10.376 --> 00:10:12.278
At least that's what a
lot of people would say.

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:16.048
- Yeah, I think some of that
depends on what, you know,

00:10:16.048 --> 00:10:17.683 align:start size:84% position:16%
where you are in the
Democratic Party.

00:10:17.683 --> 00:10:19.652
You think about the
African-American part

00:10:19.652 --> 00:10:21.120
of the Democratic Party, that,

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.222 align:start size:97% position:3%
the faith is still a
strong part of who they are

00:10:23.222 --> 00:10:24.824
and strong part of
how they identify.

00:10:24.824 --> 00:10:27.460 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I, sometimes I wonder
if the Democrat Party

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:29.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
is leaving a lot of
people of faith behind

00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:33.332
in the way they address issues.

00:10:33.332 --> 00:10:35.534 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that that would be
the fear on the Democrat side.

00:10:35.534 --> 00:10:37.703 align:start size:97% position:3%
On the Republican side, like
I said, is that this idea

00:10:37.703 --> 00:10:41.774 align:start size:97% position:3%
that some folks think, well,
I'm gonna use my politics

00:10:41.774 --> 00:10:44.710
to direct my faith instead
of the other way around.

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:47.513
- Right, so, let's talk
about that peril part,

00:10:47.513 --> 00:10:49.815
because you're talking about
the promise and the peril.

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:51.784
So what was the peril for you

00:10:51.784 --> 00:10:54.353 align:start position:0%
as you were trying to
incorporate your spiritual side

00:10:54.353 --> 00:10:55.588
to a public life?

00:10:55.588 --> 00:10:56.822 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- Well, I think this,
one of the things

00:10:56.822 --> 00:10:58.591 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
that I noticed from
being in office

00:10:58.591 --> 00:11:02.228 line:0%
was that people of faith
didn't act any different

00:11:02.228 --> 00:11:05.297
than nonbelievers in
the public square.

00:11:05.297 --> 00:11:07.733
They were just as likely to
be hateful on the internet.

00:11:07.733 --> 00:11:09.435
You talked about the
temperature rising.

00:11:09.435 --> 00:11:10.936
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:11:10.936 --> 00:11:12.738
- They were just as likely
to hide behind the anonymity

00:11:12.738 --> 00:11:16.676
to send a message that you
would never say face to face.

00:11:16.676 --> 00:11:21.213
And were just as likely
to be people, you know,

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:22.815
it says, you know,

00:11:22.815 --> 00:11:25.484 align:start size:97% position:3%
God is opposed to the proud
but gives grace to the humble.

00:11:25.484 --> 00:11:27.119
We're just as likely to be proud

00:11:27.119 --> 00:11:30.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
and think we have the
right answer on this issue

00:11:30.823 --> 00:11:35.127
as folks who were saying, we
want to get to the best answer.

00:11:35.127 --> 00:11:36.996 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think when
politics works best,

00:11:36.996 --> 00:11:39.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's about folks saying,
it's not me arguing with you

00:11:39.932 --> 00:11:41.434
about which one of us is right.

00:11:41.434 --> 00:11:44.670 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's talking with you, and
then let's figure out together

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:46.372
if we can solve this problem.

00:11:46.372 --> 00:11:47.873
- Yeah, were you
worried at times,

00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:50.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you were to talk about
your faith, not even publicly,

00:11:50.676 --> 00:11:52.278 align:start size:88% position:13%
but if you were
privately in a meeting,

00:11:52.278 --> 00:11:54.380
you've got your crew around you,

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:55.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
not even your
political opponents,

00:11:55.681 --> 00:11:57.216
but just your own
people around you,

00:11:57.216 --> 00:11:58.617
that if you were to bring
faith too much into it,

00:11:58.617 --> 00:12:00.720
that that was going
to cause a problem?

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.589
- You know, I, some of that,
just to be blunt with you,

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:05.758
it's a question of where you
are in the country, right?

00:12:05.758 --> 00:12:07.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's certain
places in Tennessee

00:12:07.193 --> 00:12:08.961 align:start position:0%
that's probably a little easier
to have that conversation

00:12:08.961 --> 00:12:10.229
than it might be in
California, right?

00:12:10.229 --> 00:12:11.497
- [Dave] Sure.

00:12:11.497 --> 00:12:13.899
- But I think that the
reality is regardless

00:12:13.899 --> 00:12:16.635
of where you live is to be
true to what you believe

00:12:16.635 --> 00:12:20.673 align:start size:94% position:6%
and not to be letting
your politics change that

00:12:20.673 --> 00:12:22.842
or affect those things that you,

00:12:22.842 --> 00:12:25.511
your most deeply held beliefs.

00:12:25.511 --> 00:12:26.846
- Yeah, are you sensing

00:12:26.846 --> 00:12:29.548 align:start size:88% position:13%
any sort of a spiritual
renewal right now?

00:12:29.548 --> 00:12:31.717
I mean, I know you mentioned
that there's a certain amount

00:12:31.717 --> 00:12:33.486 align:start size:97% position:3%
of people leaving the
church, leaving the temples,

00:12:33.486 --> 00:12:34.887
that sort of thing.

00:12:34.887 --> 00:12:37.990 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I sense a new, something
on the horizon here

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:41.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
as we're watching the secular
world just go bananas.

00:12:41.093 --> 00:12:42.595
- I actually do.

00:12:42.595 --> 00:12:44.230 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think folks look around and,
you know, it's interesting.

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:45.965
If you think about the
secular world is the one

00:12:45.965 --> 00:12:47.666
that always in the
past would say, well,

00:12:47.666 --> 00:12:50.002 align:start size:84% position:16%
there is no such
thing as truth, right?

00:12:50.002 --> 00:12:52.571 align:start size:84% position:16%
That you, that truth
is all relative,

00:12:52.571 --> 00:12:54.607
that well, you think
this, I think that,

00:12:54.607 --> 00:12:56.375 align:start size:88% position:13%
We both, I mean,
actually in California,

00:12:56.375 --> 00:12:57.777
there's a proposal
right now to say

00:12:57.777 --> 00:13:01.080
that math is not objective.

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:03.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, two plus two
doesn't equal four anymore

00:13:03.149 --> 00:13:04.650
over here in crazy Cali.

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:05.818
- Right, part of that is
this whole idea of, like,

00:13:05.818 --> 00:13:08.420 align:start size:88% position:13%
nobody can define
truth for someone else.

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:11.590 align:start position:0%
But I think we've seen in a
lot of the marches for justice,

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.225
you know, if you're
asking for justice,

00:13:13.225 --> 00:13:15.628
you're saying there is such
a thing as truth, right?

00:13:15.628 --> 00:13:18.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think what you're
seeing is a world that says,

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:21.467 align:start size:97% position:3%
if we don't have any truth,
we have some problem.

00:13:21.467 --> 00:13:23.936
And ultimately to
say there's truth

00:13:23.936 --> 00:13:28.374
means there has to be
some transcendent being

00:13:28.374 --> 00:13:31.010 align:start size:84% position:16%
who's determining,
you know, truth beyond

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:34.513 align:start size:97% position:3%
what's just the truth of this
age, if that makes sense.

00:13:34.513 --> 00:13:35.581
- No, well, it does make sense.

00:13:35.581 --> 00:13:37.349
What about the people,

00:13:37.349 --> 00:13:39.318
where do you think the people
that are the non-believers

00:13:39.318 --> 00:13:40.820
fit into this?

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.588 align:start size:97% position:3%
I, one of the things that
I've been most interested by

00:13:42.588 --> 00:13:46.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
lately is that there is a
group, Atheists for Liberty,

00:13:46.292 --> 00:13:48.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I've now seen at
some conservative events.

00:13:48.527 --> 00:13:50.930
It's, doesn't seem like
it traditionally fits

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:52.765
within the conservative tent,

00:13:52.765 --> 00:13:54.333 align:start size:81% position:19%
but the ideals
they're espousing,

00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:56.402
minus the faith part,
are those same ideals

00:13:56.402 --> 00:14:00.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
of freedom and liberty
and things of that nature.

00:14:00.172 --> 00:14:01.640 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Right, well, one of
the first things I'd say

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.777
to people of faith is
that people who say

00:14:04.777 --> 00:14:05.778
that I'm really not a believer,

00:14:05.778 --> 00:14:07.279
that they're not
the enemy, okay?

00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:10.349
That's, and, too
much, it's become,

00:14:10.349 --> 00:14:12.985
it's a good guy versus bad guys.

00:14:12.985 --> 00:14:17.122
You know, that the enemy
is all of the real issues

00:14:17.122 --> 00:14:18.657
that we're dealing with in life.

00:14:18.657 --> 00:14:20.926 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, I think, to
me, that's interesting.

00:14:20.926 --> 00:14:22.661 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like you said, I,
then, I can't remember

00:14:22.661 --> 00:14:24.630
the name of the group,
but that's a good, so -

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:25.564
- Atheists for Liberty.

00:14:25.564 --> 00:14:26.732
- You said non, right, okay.

00:14:26.732 --> 00:14:29.034 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, to me, that's
interesting, like, that

00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:31.670 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're saying there is such
a thing as liberty, okay?

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:34.173
There is such a thing that
matters in terms of freedom,

00:14:34.173 --> 00:14:35.674
which, because there
is such a thing, again,

00:14:35.674 --> 00:14:37.142
that matters in
terms of justice.

00:14:37.142 --> 00:14:40.479
And I think building on
all that is something

00:14:40.479 --> 00:14:42.715
that we would want to encourage.

00:14:42.715 --> 00:14:44.416 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Are you, I ask a
lot of my guests this,

00:14:44.416 --> 00:14:47.219 align:start size:84% position:16%
but are you hopeful
for America right now?

00:14:47.219 --> 00:14:49.021
I think related to
the faith piece,

00:14:49.021 --> 00:14:51.957 align:start position:0%
a lot of people are really,
really worried about the future.

00:14:51.957 --> 00:14:53.959
A lot of young people
just can't see a path

00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:57.229
that kind of makes
sense going forward.

00:14:57.229 --> 00:14:59.198
- So, I love your question

00:14:59.198 --> 00:15:01.433
and that's why I
wrote this book.

00:15:01.433 --> 00:15:02.902 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, you write,
I wrote this book

00:15:02.902 --> 00:15:04.203
really to people
of faith saying,

00:15:04.203 --> 00:15:06.105
the country's really
discouraged, right?

00:15:06.105 --> 00:15:07.439
Because we're divided.

00:15:07.439 --> 00:15:08.941
We're pretty equally
divided, you know.

00:15:08.941 --> 00:15:12.278 align:start size:97% position:3%
The last eight presidential
races have been close.

00:15:12.278 --> 00:15:13.512
Senate's 50, 50.

00:15:13.512 --> 00:15:14.947
The house has six
votes separating it

00:15:14.947 --> 00:15:17.917 align:start size:84% position:16%
and probably will flip
the next election.

00:15:17.917 --> 00:15:20.419
We're divided and
we're mad about it.

00:15:20.419 --> 00:15:21.687
And then, we're not even mad.

00:15:21.687 --> 00:15:22.988
We think the other
side is, you know,

00:15:22.988 --> 00:15:24.857
coming at it from bad motives,

00:15:24.857 --> 00:15:26.325
and a lot of people are
going around saying,

00:15:26.325 --> 00:15:28.093 align:start size:84% position:16%
what's going to
get us out of this?

00:15:28.093 --> 00:15:30.930
And I, my point would be,

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe here's where people
of faith, you know,

00:15:32.498 --> 00:15:34.233
scripture talks about, you know,

00:15:34.233 --> 00:15:36.435
there's salt that keeps
the meat from going bad.

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:38.671
And if the meat goes bad,
it's not the meat's fault.

00:15:38.671 --> 00:15:39.905
It's the salt's fault.

00:15:39.905 --> 00:15:41.607
And so, I'm saying
to people of faith,

00:15:41.607 --> 00:15:42.942
we should start with ourselves.

00:15:42.942 --> 00:15:44.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if we're supposed
to be the salt,

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:45.978
if the meat's going bad,

00:15:45.978 --> 00:15:48.180
if the culture seems to be
going the wrong direction,

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:50.182
maybe it's our fault.

00:15:50.182 --> 00:15:52.851
And how can we change
that conversation?

00:15:52.851 --> 00:15:54.253
- So, in essence, you would say

00:15:54.253 --> 00:15:56.588 align:start size:97% position:3%
we need to sort of spiritual
revival in some way,

00:15:56.588 --> 00:15:57.823
that the answers are not coming

00:15:57.823 --> 00:15:59.425
from getting the
right governors in

00:15:59.425 --> 00:16:01.760 align:start size:84% position:16%
with the right
marginal tax rates?

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:05.097 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, listen, I think having
good government matters.

00:16:05.097 --> 00:16:07.132
So it's not one
versus the other.

00:16:07.132 --> 00:16:08.834 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm just saying
that people of faith

00:16:08.834 --> 00:16:11.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
can be part of who
changes the conversation.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:12.871
Then back to what you said is,

00:16:12.871 --> 00:16:14.940 align:start size:84% position:16%
the temperature
continues to go up.

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:17.643 align:start size:88% position:13%
Maybe we could be people
who change the way

00:16:17.643 --> 00:16:19.411 align:start size:88% position:13%
that conversation
happens in the sense of

00:16:19.411 --> 00:16:21.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
when I'm having a
discussion with somebody,

00:16:21.513 --> 00:16:23.315
my job is not to put them down

00:16:23.315 --> 00:16:26.118 align:start size:97% position:3%
or to come up with a really
clever retort to them.

00:16:26.118 --> 00:16:28.153 align:start size:84% position:16%
But my job is to
listen and understand

00:16:28.153 --> 00:16:31.623
and say, what's the really the
best answer we can come here?

00:16:31.623 --> 00:16:33.993
Because if I really
am a person of faith,

00:16:33.993 --> 00:16:37.896
my issue here is not
to be proven right.

00:16:39.064 --> 00:16:42.368 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That's tough in an age
of social media, huh?

00:16:42.368 --> 00:16:43.736
- Yeah, no question.

00:16:43.736 --> 00:16:45.337 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's, that's
what's made it really,

00:16:45.337 --> 00:16:48.107
people say what's different
today from 30 years ago?

00:16:48.107 --> 00:16:51.043 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'd say 30 years ago, we
didn't have this environment

00:16:51.043 --> 00:16:54.513
that rewards people for
being social media stars

00:16:54.513 --> 00:16:56.382
instead of actually
getting things done.

00:16:56.382 --> 00:16:58.417
It's like, till we have a
lot of people in Washington

00:16:58.417 --> 00:17:00.185 align:start size:84% position:16%
who are like, I'm
not really a senator.

00:17:00.185 --> 00:17:02.855
I just play one on TV
or on social media.

00:17:02.855 --> 00:17:05.357 align:start position:0%
And, as you know, it's real
easy to be a social media star.

00:17:05.357 --> 00:17:10.295 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, the hopeful thing is
maybe we can encourage people

00:17:10.295 --> 00:17:13.165 align:start size:94% position:6%
who want to say, I'm
here not to make a point,

00:17:13.165 --> 00:17:15.934
but to make a difference
in a world that gives you

00:17:15.934 --> 00:17:17.870
a lot of applause
for making a point.

00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:19.705
- Yeah, do you think some
of this is just the fact

00:17:19.705 --> 00:17:21.407
that so many people
have a voice now,

00:17:21.407 --> 00:17:23.175
that part of the chaos is

00:17:23.175 --> 00:17:24.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
just that we're
getting more voices,

00:17:24.676 --> 00:17:26.545
where in the old days we just
didn't have all the voices,

00:17:26.545 --> 00:17:29.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so we sort of thought
we were more unified,

00:17:29.348 --> 00:17:30.916
but maybe we weren't?

00:17:30.916 --> 00:17:31.850
- I think that's fair.

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:33.085
And people always say, you know,

00:17:33.085 --> 00:17:35.721
what are the party
fathers and mothers

00:17:35.721 --> 00:17:36.789
of the Republican Party?

00:17:36.789 --> 00:17:38.023
What are they doing about this?

00:17:38.023 --> 00:17:38.957
Which, I said, you
know, there are,

00:17:38.957 --> 00:17:40.459
there is no such thing anymore.

00:17:40.459 --> 00:17:43.929
I mean, you know, the, there
is no backroom group of folks

00:17:43.929 --> 00:17:46.331 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
who are deciding,
making these decisions.

00:17:46.331 --> 00:17:48.600 line:0%
It's become a very flat world,

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:50.235 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and everybody is
getting their voice.

00:17:50.235 --> 00:17:53.272 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
So I do think there is, some
of that is this is just,

00:17:53.272 --> 00:17:57.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
everybody has their own
microphone and megaphone now.

00:17:57.209 --> 00:17:59.978
But I think that that
cacophony of noise

00:17:59.978 --> 00:18:01.880
has led folks to saying, well,

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:05.184 align:start size:97% position:3%
how do we ever get direction
and vision out of this?

00:18:05.184 --> 00:18:07.786
- Yeah, well, I want to
give you the last word.

00:18:07.786 --> 00:18:09.054
You've been hopeful throughout.

00:18:09.054 --> 00:18:10.722
One of the things I've
try to do on my show,

00:18:10.722 --> 00:18:12.224 align:start size:84% position:16%
on my daily show and
in my interviews,

00:18:12.224 --> 00:18:13.492
I want people to feel hope,

00:18:13.492 --> 00:18:15.794
because this is so
screwy right now,

00:18:15.794 --> 00:18:18.997
and a lot of people are
feeling kinda depressed.

00:18:20.165 --> 00:18:21.700
Give me one more
pitch for for hope

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:25.204
and how people that want
to be involved politically,

00:18:25.204 --> 00:18:29.508
how, in your view, their faith
can help them in that regard.

00:18:29.508 --> 00:18:30.776
- Well, I would say this.

00:18:30.776 --> 00:18:32.978
Don't give up on the
public square, okay?

00:18:32.978 --> 00:18:36.014
As messy as it is, as
frustrating as it is,

00:18:36.014 --> 00:18:39.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the public square
is still that place

00:18:39.218 --> 00:18:42.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
where you can affect
the common good,

00:18:42.521 --> 00:18:46.191
that you, this is where you can
really make the most change.

00:18:46.191 --> 00:18:48.093
And, I left office.

00:18:48.093 --> 00:18:50.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was a mayor for seven years,
a governor for eight years.

00:18:50.395 --> 00:18:54.099 align:start size:97% position:3%
I left office after 15 years
being way more impressed

00:18:54.099 --> 00:18:57.536 align:start size:97% position:3%
with how many people
really want to get it right

00:18:57.536 --> 00:18:59.304
and how many people truly care.

00:18:59.304 --> 00:19:02.007
Unfortunately, there's
some really loud voices

00:19:02.007 --> 00:19:04.042
that are interested
in being loud voices

00:19:04.042 --> 00:19:05.444 align:start size:88% position:13%
instead of
accomplishing something.

00:19:05.444 --> 00:19:07.980
But, my sense is,
there's a great,

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:09.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't know if I want to
say great majority of people,

00:19:09.481 --> 00:19:12.918
but there's a large number of
people who really truly care

00:19:12.918 --> 00:19:14.920
and want this to be
about being better.

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:17.456
- Yeah, I agree, and we just
need those people to speak up.

00:19:17.456 --> 00:19:19.224
It's almost as simple as that.

00:19:20.859 --> 00:19:23.495 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Thanks, I really enjoyed,
I enjoyed our visit,

00:19:23.495 --> 00:19:25.130
and I really appreciate
having the opportunity

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:26.198
to be on your show.

00:19:26.198 --> 00:19:28.534
